{
 "esm": "1.1.0",
 "metadata": {
  "name": "FireWindHinterpWRFFire",
  "description": "The horizontal atmosphere-to-fire mapping of NCAR fire_behavior (share/interp_mod.F90 Interp_horizontal_bilinear, reached through io/coupling_mod.F90 Interp_horizontal and io/wrfdata_mod.F90 Interp_var2grid at hinterp_opt = 2, the default): the mid-flame winds that Calc_fire_wind produces PER ATMOSPHERE COLUMN are interpolated bilinearly onto the fire mesh, with nearest-neighbour extrapolation outside the atmosphere extent. The interpolation itself is the EarthSciAST closed function interp.bilinear, called in SOURCE-INDEX space: the axes are the atmosphere grid's own index axes 1..GX_SRC and 1..GY_SRC (uniform integers, built from the index sets so the component follows its metaparameters), and every bit of projection curvature lives in the per-cell target coordinates i_real and j_real. WHY interp.bilinear AND NOT A REGRIDDING RULE, so nobody re-derives one: a hand-rolled EarthSciDiscretizations entry (regridding/cartesian_bilinear_regrid, method:bilinear, with _base/_offset/_apply stage templates) was written first and then withdrawn, because the engine primitive turned out to be the SAME FUNCTION. Measured, not assumed: the two agree to within 1e-15 at every one of the 6400 fire cells for both wind components; against the Fortran both give the identical full-field residuals uf 1.084e-7 and vf 4.353e-7; and at the two clamped corners both return the edge value exactly. interp.bilinear's documented clamp clamps the COORDINATE to the axis range, which for a bilinear interpolant yields exactly the edge value -- the same thing Fortran's clamp of the base index and the fractional offset does. It accepts a non-constant table, which the coupling hook below needs, and it vectorises inside an aggregate over the target grid with per-cell scalar coordinates. Nothing about the fire case needed a library entry; the only thing a rule would have added is an obligation to maintain a conformance fixture for a duplicate of an engine primitive. WHY IT HAD TO EXIST. FireWindWRFFire is verified at rel 1e-5 but could not be MOUNTED in couplings/wildlandfire_behavior.esm, because its output lives on the atmosphere grid and the fire cell the coupled document is built on needs a fire-mesh value. The two are not the same number and no index mapping makes them so: the 80 x 80 fire mesh of test7 carries 5593 distinct uf values against 361 atmosphere columns, and the nearest column to fire cell (18, 64) differs from it by 2.2e-3 m/s, about 200 times that component's tolerance. This component is the missing link. THE FRACTIONAL COORDINATES ARE GEOMETRY, NOT A REFINEMENT RATIO, and that is the substantive finding here. The fire mesh is built by subdividing the atmosphere grid, so one expects the fractional source coordinate of fire cell i to be affine, i_real = (i - 1/2)/sr + 1/2. It is not: the mapping is proj%Calc_ij, the inverse Lambert conformal projection applied to each fire cell's latitude and longitude, and the projection's curvature over this small domain already breaks affineness. MEASURED on test7 step 20: with the dumped i_real/j_real the bilinear reproduces the Fortran to 1.08e-7 absolute, 1.2e-7 relative; with the best least-squares AFFINE fit to those same coordinates it is 1.07e-3, and with the textbook sr map 1.19e-3 -- both about 120 times the rel 1e-5 this component is held to. So i_real and j_real are consumer-supplied geometry, exactly as the column components take heights and pressures rather than deriving them, and sr_x/sr_y appear nowhere. A consumer that wants them from first principles composes EarthSciDiscretizations reprojection/lambert_conformal with the fire mesh's latitudes and longitudes; they are time-invariant, so in practice they are read once from geo_em. Reference: fire_behavior test7 standalone at step 20, real32, rel 1e-5, from the `hinterp` dump hook added in fork ctessum-claude/fire_behavior@22d426e. COUPLING HOOKS: `i_coupled`/`j_coupled` with `u_src_in`/`v_src_in` replace ONE atmosphere column's wind with a coupling target, so a mounted FireWindWRFFire can drive it (0 disables, so a standalone run is unchanged); and `uf_cell`/`vf_cell` expose the interpolated wind at fire cell (`i_cell`, `j_cell`) as scalars, which is what a per-cell consumer such as ROSWRFFire needs. RUN-TIME TABLE (2026-10-16): the table here is the time-evolving wind field u_src_eff / v_src_eff and the axes are built from the index sets, and esm-spec section 9.2 admits only literal const arrays as interp.bilinear's table and axes (interp_table_not_const / interp_axis_not_const; the Rust native compiler refuses the variable form). That rule stands (decided 2026-10-16, the question EarthSciAST #443 raised), so the interpolation is written with lib/table_interp.esm: interp.bilinear's semantics spelled out -- bracket each source coordinate by counting the axis entries at or below it (i_bracket, j_bracket, shared by u and v), clamp it to its axis, gather the four corners with interp_bilinear_bracketed, and blend in section 9.2's pinned order -- which is bit-identical to the closed function. Everything said above about interp.bilinear holds for it unchanged.",
  "license": "MIT",
  "tags": [
   "wildland_fire",
   "fire_behavior",
   "regridding",
   "interpolation"
  ],
  "references": [
   {
    "citation": "NCAR Community Fire Behavior Model (fire_behavior), develop @ eb77580, share/interp_mod.F90 Interp_horizontal_bilinear; instrumented fork ctessum-claude/fire_behavior branch earthsciml-instrumented (the hinterp dump hook)."
   }
  ]
 },
 "metaparameters": {
  "NX": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 80,
   "description": "Fire-mesh cells in x."
  },
  "NY": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 80,
   "description": "Fire-mesh cells in y."
  },
  "GX_SRC": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 19,
   "description": "Atmosphere-grid columns in x (test7 ide - ids = 19)."
  },
  "GY_SRC": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 19,
   "description": "Atmosphere-grid columns in y."
  }
 },
 "models": {
  "FireWindHinterpWRFFire": {
   "expression_template_imports": [
    {
     "ref": "${ESD_ROOT}/grids/cartesian_uniform_2d/grid.esm",
     "bindings": {
      "NX": "NX",
      "NY": "NY"
     }
    },
    {
     "ref": "../../../lib/table_interp.esm"
    }
   ],
   "variables": {
    "x0": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.0,
     "description": "West edge of the fire mesh (cartesian_uniform_2d geometry contract; unused here because no derivative is taken)."
    },
    "y0": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.0,
     "description": "South edge of the fire mesh."
    },
    "Lx": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 2000.0,
     "description": "Fire-mesh extent in x (test7: 80 cells of 25 m)."
    },
    "Ly": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 2000.0,
     "description": "Fire-mesh extent in y."
    },
    "dx": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "description": "Cell size in x, Lx/NX."
    },
    "dy": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "description": "Cell size in y, Ly/NY."
    },
    "u_src": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "shape": [
      "src_x",
      "src_y"
     ],
     "description": "Mid-flame wind u on the ATMOSPHERE grid, one value per atmosphere column (fire_behavior ua, the output of Calc_fire_wind). Consumer-supplied through `input_u_src`."
    },
    "v_src": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "shape": [
      "src_x",
      "src_y"
     ],
     "description": "Mid-flame wind v on the atmosphere grid (fire_behavior va)."
    },
    "i_real": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "shape": [
      "x",
      "y"
     ],
     "description": "GEOMETRY, consumer-supplied through `input_i_real`: the fractional atmosphere-grid coordinate of each fire cell along the first axis, i.e. what proj%Calc_ij returns for that cell's latitude and longitude. It is NOT an affine function of the fire index -- see the component description."
    },
    "j_real": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "shape": [
      "x",
      "y"
     ],
     "description": "GEOMETRY, consumer-supplied through `input_j_real`: the fractional atmosphere-grid coordinate along the second axis."
    },
    "i_bracket": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "shape": [
      "x",
      "y"
     ],
     "description": "Lower bracketing index of each fire cell's source coordinate i_real on src_axis_x: the number of axis entries at or below it, held to [1, GX_SRC-1] (lib/table_interp.esm bracket_index). Shared by the u and v lookups."
    },
    "j_bracket": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "shape": [
      "x",
      "y"
     ],
     "description": "Lower bracketing index of j_real on src_axis_y, held to [1, GY_SRC-1]; shared by the u and v lookups."
    },
    "uf": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "shape": [
      "x",
      "y"
     ],
     "description": "Mid-flame wind u on the FIRE mesh (fire_behavior uf), the bilinear interpolation of u_src at (i_real, j_real)."
    },
    "vf": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "shape": [
      "x",
      "y"
     ],
     "description": "Mid-flame wind v on the fire mesh (fire_behavior vf)."
    },
    "i_coupled": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 0.0,
     "description": "Coupling hook: first-axis index of the ATMOSPHERE column whose wind is taken from u_src_in/v_src_in instead of the supplied field; 0 disables the replacement, so a standalone run is unchanged. Same idiom as s_coupled in fire_flux_to_atmosphere_wrffire.esm and i_coupled in level_set_tendency_wrffire.esm."
    },
    "j_coupled": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 0.0,
     "description": "Second-axis index of that column."
    },
    "u_src_in": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m/s",
     "default": 0.0,
     "description": "Coupling target: the u wind of column (i_coupled, j_coupled), supplied by a mounted FireWindWRFFire."
    },
    "v_src_in": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m/s",
     "default": 0.0,
     "description": "Coupling target: the v wind of that column."
    },
    "i_cell": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 1.0,
     "description": "First-axis index of the FIRE cell whose interpolated wind is exposed as the scalars uf_cell/vf_cell, so a consumer can drive a per-cell scheme such as ROSWRFFire without handling the whole field."
    },
    "j_cell": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 1.0,
     "description": "Second-axis index of that fire cell."
    },
    "u_src_eff": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "shape": [
      "src_x",
      "src_y"
     ],
     "description": "The atmosphere u field with column (i_coupled, j_coupled) replaced by u_src_in."
    },
    "v_src_eff": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "shape": [
      "src_x",
      "src_y"
     ],
     "description": "The atmosphere v field with that column replaced by v_src_in."
    },
    "uf_cell": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Interpolated u wind at fire cell (i_cell, j_cell)."
    },
    "vf_cell": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m/s",
     "description": "Interpolated v wind at that fire cell."
    },
    "src_axis_x": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "shape": [
      "src_x"
     ],
     "description": "The atmosphere grid's own index axis along x, 1, 2, ..., GX_SRC -- uniform integers, because the interpolation works in SOURCE-INDEX space. It is built from the index set rather than written as a constant so the component follows the GX_SRC metaparameter."
    },
    "src_axis_y": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "shape": [
      "src_y"
     ],
     "description": "The atmosphere grid's own index axis along y, 1, 2, ..., GY_SRC -- uniform integers, because the interpolation works in SOURCE-INDEX space. It is built from the index set rather than written as a constant so the component follows the GY_SRC metaparameter."
    }
   },
